        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Reach for a <strong>rust converter</strong> when the metal is large, fixed in place or full of pits you will never fully clean, and it is going to be painted. Trailer frames, iron railings, truck underbodies, steel fence posts, gutters and basement support columns are classic converter jobs. You knock off the loose scale, brush on the converter, let it turn black, then topcoat it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reach for a <strong>rust remover</strong> when the part must go back to bare metal: hand tools, cast-iron pans, threaded fasteners, engine and machine parts, antique hardware, anything with close tolerances, and any panel that will get a quality automotive finish. Small parts go in a soak bath; larger surfaces get a gel, a wire wheel or blasting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many projects use both. A restorer might strip a bumper bracket in a soak bath, then use converter on the pitted inside of the frame rail it bolts to. The question to ask of each surface is simple: does it need to be clean metal, or does it just need to stop rusting and hold paint?
          </p>

