        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Convert when the metal is big, fixed, pitted and going to be painted. Strip when it is small enough to soak, needs to function precisely, or will get a show-quality finish. For most garages, a jug of <strong>Evapo-Rust</strong> for parts and a can of <strong>Rust-Oleum Rust Reformer</strong> or a quart of <strong>Corroseal</strong> for larger surfaces covers nearly every job.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Either way, prep and follow-through decide the result. Remove the loose rust, clean off grease, and protect the surface right away with oil, primer or paint. If the rust you are fighting is in a damp basement, stop the moisture too, then finish the space with a floor that tolerates it, such as our <a href="/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/">vinyl plank picks</a>.
          </p>
